<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  2026-08-20 (owner's decision): the app is deliberately NOT sandboxed.

	  #19 added com.apple.security.app-sandbox here on the theory that App Group
	  storage only redirects into ~/Library/Group Containers/ for a sandboxed
	  process, so an unsandboxed app and a sandboxed widget would be reading two
	  different files. Measured on the shipped build, that is not what happens:
	  the 2026-07-28 build carried this entitlement WITHOUT app-sandbox and still
	  read and wrote the widget's container. The App Group entitlement alone is
	  what moves the suite.

	  What the sandbox did cost was real. PomoLight is distributed directly and
	  free, never through the App Store, so nothing requires it — and turning it
	  on relocates UserDefaults.standard into the app's private container, which
	  silently reset every stored setting on the owner's Mac (theme, alarm voice,
	  volume, repeat, and the "show seconds" toggle that made the loss visible).
	  macOS does not migrate the old file across, and a sandboxed app cannot read
	  it back without a temporary-exception entitlement. Notarization is unaffected
	  either way — that needs the hardened runtime, which project.yml sets.

	  The widget keeps its own app-sandbox: ExtensionKit extensions require it.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	<array>
		<string>group.com.vismo.pomolight</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
